WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.257
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book four.

2
00:00:05.557 --> 00:00:09.234
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.534 --> 00:00:15.530
Unit one: Business Strategy. Today you will evaluate strategic choices with comparative structures.

4
00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:18.459
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:22.435
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.283 --> 00:00:24.110
Strategy.

7
00:00:25.117 --> 00:00:29.961
Strategy means a long-term plan for achieving an organisation's goals.

8
00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:33.385
Their strategy is to dominate rural mobile banking.

9
00:00:33.983 --> 00:00:36.856
Your turn. Say: Strategy.

10
00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:40.771
Strategy.

11
00:00:41.517 --> 00:00:43.226
Competitive advantage.

12
00:00:44.217 --> 00:00:48.689
Competitive advantage means a quality that lets a firm outperform its rivals.

13
00:00:49.283 --> 00:00:52.630
Low-cost logistics gave the firm a competitive advantage.

14
00:00:53.217 --> 00:00:56.728
Your turn. Say: Competitive advantage.

15
00:00:59.567 --> 00:01:00.893
Competitive advantage.

16
00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:02.930
Market position.

17
00:01:03.917 --> 00:01:08.575
Market position means how a firm stands relative to competitors in customers' minds.

18
00:01:09.183 --> 00:01:12.506
The bank strengthened its market position among young savers.

19
00:01:13.117 --> 00:01:15.955
Your turn. Say: Market position.

20
00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:20.033
Market position.

21
00:01:20.783 --> 00:01:22.179
Value proposition.

22
00:01:23.183 --> 00:01:27.749
Value proposition means the specific benefit a product promises its customers.

23
00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:31.394
Their value proposition is 'transfers in three seconds'.

24
00:01:31.983 --> 00:01:35.228
Your turn. Say: Value proposition.

25
00:01:38.067 --> 00:01:39.555
Value proposition.

26
00:01:40.317 --> 00:01:41.585
Differentiation.

27
00:01:42.583 --> 00:01:46.905
Differentiation means making a product distinct from competitors' offerings.

28
00:01:47.517 --> 00:01:50.805
Differentiation through design justified the higher price.

29
00:01:51.417 --> 00:01:54.789
Your turn. Say: Differentiation.

30
00:01:57.633 --> 00:01:58.809
Differentiation.

31
00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:00.365
Vision.

32
00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:05.509
Vision means a clear picture of what an organisation wants to become.

33
00:02:06.117 --> 00:02:08.708
The founder's vision was a cashless Cambodia.

34
00:02:09.317 --> 00:02:11.888
Your turn. Say: Vision.

35
00:02:14.733 --> 00:02:15.607
Vision.

36
00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:17.653
Execution.

37
00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:22.078
Execution means the practical implementation of a plan.

38
00:02:22.683 --> 00:02:26.215
A brilliant plan is worthless without disciplined execution.

39
00:02:26.817 --> 00:02:29.341
Your turn. Say: Execution.

40
00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:33.515
Execution.

41
00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:35.123
Growth.

42
00:02:36.117 --> 00:02:39.707
Growth means an increase in a company's size, sales or profits.

43
00:02:40.317 --> 00:02:43.384
Rapid growth can strain a company's cash reserves.

44
00:02:43.983 --> 00:02:46.415
Your turn. Say: Growth.

45
00:02:49.267 --> 00:02:50.047
Growth.

46
00:02:50.833 --> 00:02:54.658
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:55.517 --> 00:03:02.082
Visal, two coffee chains opened on the same street in Phnom Penh. Why is one thriving while the other is struggling?

48
00:03:03.183 --> 00:03:14.137
Because its value proposition is far clearer. It promises fast, consistent coffee for office workers, which is stronger than simply claiming to be 'the best'.

49
00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:17.714
But the struggling chain has lower prices.

50
00:03:18.817 --> 00:03:28.087
Low prices are easier to copy than a reputation for reliability. A cost advantage is rarely as durable as genuine differentiation.

51
00:03:29.183 --> 00:03:32.205
So strategy is mainly about choosing what not to do?

52
00:03:33.317 --> 00:03:45.399
Exactly. The more focused the strategy, the greater the chance of disciplined execution. Trying to please everyone is weaker than serving one segment brilliantly.

53
00:03:46.533 --> 00:03:48.161
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:49.017 --> 00:03:53.408
Grammar focus: Comparative structures (stronger than, greater than).

55
00:03:53.708 --> 00:03:54.941
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:58.683
A clear value proposition is stronger than a vague one.

57
00:03:59.583 --> 00:04:03.220
The risk of copying rivals is far greater than the risk of focusing.

58
00:04:04.117 --> 00:04:07.626
Differentiation is often more durable than low prices.

59
00:04:08.567 --> 00:04:16.200
Modify comparatives to sound precise: far / considerably / slightly stronger than. Use 'the more…, the greater…' to link two changing qualities.

60
00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:20.903
Be careful! Don't say: Quality is more important that price.

61
00:04:22.867 --> 00:04:25.702
Say: Quality is more important than price.

62
00:04:26.002 --> 00:04:27.607
Comparatives use 'than'.

63
00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:31.203
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:33.280
Choosing Where to Win.

65
00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:38.482
Business strategy is less about doing everything well than about deciding where and how to compete.

66
00:04:39.467 --> 00:04:46.888
Michael Porter argued that a firm achieves competitive advantage either by lowering costs or by offering something customers value more highly.

67
00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:56.866
Cambodian mobile banking illustrates the point: providers that focused on speed and simplicity grew far faster than those offering long lists of features.

68
00:04:57.867 --> 00:05:05.578
Nevertheless, a strategy is only as strong as its execution, since even a brilliant plan collapses when staff do not understand it.

69
00:05:06.567 --> 00:05:11.945
The most successful firms therefore combine a sharp vision with disciplined, measurable action.

70
00:05:13.033 --> 00:05:16.977
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:17.817 --> 00:05:21.337
A focused plan is often blank than an unfocused one.

72
00:05:21.637 --> 00:05:25.453
A, strong. B, more strong. C, stronger.

73
00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:30.965
The answer is C: stronger.

74
00:05:32.117 --> 00:05:35.637
The specific benefit a product promises customers is its blank.

75
00:05:35.937 --> 00:05:39.997
A, value proposition. B, execution. C, growth.

76
00:05:43.533 --> 00:05:46.067
The answer is A: value proposition.

77
00:05:47.217 --> 00:05:50.505
Making a product distinct from rivals' offerings is blank.

78
00:05:50.805 --> 00:05:55.410
A, vision. B, differentiation. C, market position.

79
00:05:58.967 --> 00:06:01.512
The answer is B: differentiation.

80
00:06:02.650 --> 00:06:04.266
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:06:04.566 --> 00:06:12.713
A, Quality is more important that price.. B, Quality is far more important than price.. C, Quality is most important than price..

82
00:06:16.267 --> 00:06:20.414
The answer is B: Quality is far more important than price..

83
00:06:21.550 --> 00:06:25.488
According to the reading, a strategy is only as strong as its…

84
00:06:25.788 --> 00:06:31.276
A, advertising budget. B, execution. C, number of features.

85
00:06:34.833 --> 00:06:36.961
The answer is B: execution.

86
00:06:38.167 --> 00:06:41.516
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:42.350 --> 00:06:45.882
What gives a company a real competitive advantage in its market?

88
00:07:02.683 --> 00:07:04.265
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:07:04.565 --> 00:07:41.774
A real competitive advantage comes from something customers value that rivals find difficult to copy. Low prices can attract buyers quickly, but they are far easier to imitate than a strong reputation, a loyal community or unique know-how. In Cambodia, for example, the most successful payment apps won because they were simpler and more reliable than the alternatives. However, any advantage fades without excellent execution, so I believe disciplined teams are ultimately stronger than clever slogans.

90
00:07:42.074 --> 00:07:43.760
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:44.650 --> 00:07:49.996
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and comparative structures (stronger than, greater than).

92
00:07:50.296 --> 00:07:56.512
Today's value is strategic vision. See the bigger picture, and plan for where you want to be.

93
00:07:56.812 --> 00:07:59.279
See you in Unit two. Goodbye!

94
00:07:59.579 --> 00:08:02.127
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

